---
import Links from "../../components/Links.astro";
import BlogLayout from "../../layouts/BlogLayout.astro";

import { Picture } from "astro:assets";
import TermGifPlayerGif from "../../images/terminal-gif-player.gif";

const title = "Terminal GIF Player";
---

<BlogLayout
    title={title}
    description="Terminal GIF Player plays high-definition GIFs in the terminal."
>
    <Fragment slot="header">
        <div class="grid justify-center">
            <a href={TermGifPlayerGif.src} aria-label="Open image">
                <Picture
                    src={TermGifPlayerGif}
                    alt="Terminal GIF Player playing a GIF of the famous donut-shaped C code that outputs a 3D spinning donut."
                    loading="eager"
                    layout="full-width"
                />
            </a>
        </div>
        <h1>{title}</h1>
        <Links
            links={[
                {
                    href: "https://codeberg.org/TemariVirus/terminal-gif-player",
                    text: "Code",
                },
            ]}
        />
    </Fragment>

    <p>Terminal GIF Player plays high-definition GIFs in the terminal.</p>
    <p>
        I made this as an experiment to see how I could push "rendering" stuff
        in the terminal with <a
            href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/ANSI_escape_code#24-bit"
            >ANSI escape codes</a
        >.
    </p>
    <p>
        I learned that I of course was not the first person to try this, and
        picked up many tricks from others to make the program render 10_000s of
        pixels per frame. These include:
    </p>
    <ul>
        <li>
            Using ANSI escape codes to move the cursor and only draw pixels that
            changed since the last frame.
        </li>
        <li>
            Using the "▀" character to render 2 pixels per character, one with
            the foreground color, and another with the background color.
        </li>
        <li>Buffering writes to stdout.</li>
    </ul>
</BlogLayout>
